{"name":"Wind Force","hex":"#d5e2ee","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wind force","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["the strength of the wind. The Beaufort scale was devised to classify wind forces of various strengths."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind_force"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beaufort scale","blocks":[{"text":"The Beaufort scale is an empirical measure that relates wind speed to observed conditions at sea or on land. Its full name is the Beaufort wind force scale. It was devised in 1805 by Francis Beaufort, a hydrographer in the British Royal Navy. It was officially adopted by the Royal Navy and later spread internationally."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Beaufort_scale_12_notext.jpg/330px-Beaufort_scale_12_notext.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaufort_scale"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind"},{"title":"Force","blocks":[{"text":"In physics, a force is an action that can cause an object to change its velocity or its shape, or to resist other forces, or to cause changes of pressure in a fluid. In mechanics, force makes ideas like pushing or pulling mathematically precise. Because the magnitude and direction of a force are both important, force is a vector quantity. The SI unit of force is the newton (N), and force is often represented by the symbol F."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Force_examples.svg/330px-Force_examples.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Force"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/FrancisBeaufort.jpg/500px-FrancisBeaufort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/FrancisBeaufort.jpg/500px-FrancisBeaufort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sir Francis Beaufort","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrancisBeaufort.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Beaufort_wind_scale.png/500px-Beaufort_wind_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Beaufort_wind_scale.png/500px-Beaufort_wind_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Data graphic showing Beaufort wind force in scale units, knots and metres per second","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeaufort_wind_scale.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Beaufort_scale_0.jpg/330px-Beaufort_scale_0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Beaufort_scale_0.jpg/330px-Beaufort_scale_0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeaufort_scale_0.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Beaufort_scale_1.jpg/330px-Beaufort_scale_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb